From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 14:33:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(process): drain a PTY child's buffered output after it
 exits [daveey]

The PTY reader loop ran `while pty.isalive()`, and isalive() turns False as soon as
the child is reaped. A reader thread starved of CPU from spawn to the child's exit
therefore left the loop without reading anything and published an exited session
with an empty output, though the child's bytes were still in the master buffer.

That is the CI flake of
tests/tools/test_process_registry.py::TestStdinHelpers::test_close_stdin_allows_eof_driven_process_to_finish
(run 43582221114, job 132409314320: exit_code 0, `assert 'hello' in ''`).

Five whys:
1. The poll returned exited/0 with output_preview == "" -> the reader never ingested the child's output.
2. It never read it -> the loop condition isalive() was already False on the reader's first check.
3. isalive() was False -> the child had written "hello" and exited before the reader thread ran.
4. The reader still exited the loop -> nothing drained the master after the loop; only the decoder tail was flushed.
5. No drain existed -> the loop assumed it would always observe output before exit, true only on a quiet host.

Fix: after the loop (POSIX), read whatever the master holds right now through the
shared fd_wait selector with a zero timeout, capped at the existing
_FINAL_DRAIN_MAX_CHARS. Only already-readable bytes are read, so a descendant that
keeps the slave open cannot hold up completion. Chunk handling (query responder,
decoder, ingest) moved into one local function shared by the loop and the drain;
tools/process_registry.py stays at its line count.

Proof:
- tests/tools/test_process_registry_pty_drain.py gates the reader's first isalive()
  until the child has exited (os.waitid WNOWAIT, child stays unreaped): RED on base
  with the exact CI assertion `assert 'hello' in ''`, GREEN with the fix.
- process_registry, pty_kill, submit, write_stdin_surrogates, completion_races,
  pty_query_responder and the new file: 147 passed, 4 skipped.
- SIGSTOP/SIGCONT duty-cycle (40 ms run / 160 ms stop) of the original test: 12/12 pass.
- scripts/check --base origin/main: ok.
